          %title Supergeometry
          %blockquote
            %p
              this entry is one section of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+supergeometry+and+superphysics"}> geometry of physics – supergeometry and superphysics
              ”
            %p
              which is one chapter of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"}> geometry of physics
              ”
            %p
              previous sections:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+superalgebra"} superalgebra
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+categories+and+toposes"} categories and toposes
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            %p
              following sections:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+supersymmetry"} geometry of physics – supersymmetry
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+homotopy+types"} geometry of physics – smooth homotopy types
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} Supergeometry
            is the generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+functions"} algebras of functions
            are generalized from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebras"} commutative algebras
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercommutative+superalgebras"} supercommutative superalgebras
          %p
            In
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+superalgebra"} geometry of physics – superalgebra
            we discussed why it is mathematically compelling to pass to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercommutative+superalgebra"} supercommutative superalgebra
            and how this implies a dual concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspace"} superspace
            in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+superschemes"} affine superschemes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            on these affine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspaces"} superspaces
            –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            – in parallel to the discussion of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 20px 10px 20px;"}
            %a{:href => "#ProgressionOfIdempotentEndofunctors"}
              %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/schreiber/files/ProgressionOfModalities.jpg", :width => "200"}/
          %p
            Apart from the abstract mathematical motivation for supergeometry, it is also an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experimental+observation"} experimental
            fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            is fundamentally described by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            (already of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            (such as that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspace"} superspace
            whose even-graded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            are the configurations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boson"} boson
            fields, while the odd-graded coordinates correspond to the configurations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            fields. It is impossible to have an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for fermion fields as a function on a non-super (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifold"}> infinite-dimensional
            )-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"}> manifold
            \.
          %p
            This is an old insight: The experimental detection of the special properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            that show their super-geometric nature goes back all the way to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stern-Gerlach+experiment"} Stern-Gerlach experiment
            in 1922, which revealed that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors"} spinors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            (
            %a{:href => "#Pauli25"}> Pauli 1925
            ) –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction"} deduced
            from the nature of energy levels of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atoms"} atoms
            – says that no two such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors"} spinors
            may occupy the exact same
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            has to satisfy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\psi \psi = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            A little later it was realized that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            %em imply
            that all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            fields need to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            in that for any two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            spinor field variables
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_2
            the Grassmann sign rule holds (
            %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+-superalgebra#Grassmann1844"}> Grassmann 1844
            ):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\psi_1 \psi_2 = - \psi_2 \psi_1
          %p
            (which of course immediately implies the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            %a{:href => "#Fierz39"} Fierz in 1939
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Pauli40"} Pauli in 1940
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercommutative+superalgebra"} supercommutative superalgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_i
            being odd-graded functions on a supergeometric
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
          %p
            Notice that this phenomenon is not a negligible subtlety: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            is what implies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stability+of+matter"} stability of matter
            by forcing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom"} atom
            to fill up “orbitals” consecutively as opposed to all falling into the ground state, as a bosonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensate"} condensate
            would do. There would be no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            without supergeometry of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solidity"} solid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/degeneracy+pressure"} degeneracy pressure
            controls more exotic phenomena, for instance the stability of neutron stars against their gravitational collapse.)
          %p As a slogan:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,\,\,\,\,\;
            %em
              %strong
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,\,\,\,\,\;
            %em
              %strong
                hence for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
                fields admitting
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid states
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,\,\,\,\,\;
            %em
              %strong
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
          %p
            Nevertheless, few textbooks make the supergeometric aspect in the physics of fermions explicit. Some discuss it only after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            (and some will even claim that fermion fields do not exist classically); some only discuss it in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            is a subject in itself even without presence or mentioning of spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            (which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is a subject in itself even without the presence or mentioning of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            symmetry. Hence this we discuss independently in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+supersymmetry"} geometry of physics – supersymmetry
          %p
            Texts which do make the super-geometric nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion+fields"} fermion fields
            explicit include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#DeligneFreed99"}> Deligne-Freed 99, §3.4
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Freed01"} Freed 01, lecture 4
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#GiachettaMangiarottiSardanashvily09"}> Giachetta-Mangiarotti-Sardanashvily 09, chapter 3
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Sardanashvily12"}> Sardanashvily 12
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Sardanashvily16"} Sardanashvily 16
          %p This we discuss elsewhere.
          %p
