          %h1#supergeometry_2 Supergeometry
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#CoordinareSystemsSuperCartesianSpaces"} Super Cartesian spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#SuperSmoothSets"} Super smooth sets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#SuperMappingSpaces"} Super mapping spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Supermanifolds"} Supermanifolds
              %li
                %a{:href => "#DeRhamComplexOfSuperDifferentialForms"} Super differential forms
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general_theory"} General theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#for_classical_field_theories_with_fermions"} For classical field theories with fermions
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesOverSuperpoints"} In the topos over superpoints
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_the_topos_over_super_cartesian_spaces"} In the topos over super Cartesian spaces
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            In the section
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+superalgebra"} geometry of physics – superalgebra
            we had discussed the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+super+schemes"} affine super schemes
            (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+superalgebra#AffineSuperSchemes"} this definition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Var
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi sVect
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msubsup
                    %mi sCalg
                    %mi k
                    %mi op
                  %mo =
                  %mi CMon
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi sVect
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Var(sVect_{\mathbb{R}}) \coloneqq sCalg_k^{op} = CMon(sVect_{\mathbb{R}})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here we use these as local model spaces to develop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            locally modeled on affine superschemes. Since we are interested in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            in physics are objects in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            (even if sometimes they may come from there), we consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartesian+spaces"} super Cartesian spaces
            (
            %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+superalgebra#SuperCartesianSpace"}> this example
            )
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SuperCartSp
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi q
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Aff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi sVect
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  SuperCartSp = \{\mathbb{R}^{p\vert q}\}_{p,q \in \mathbb{N}} \hookrightarrow Aff(sVect_k)
                  \\,,
          %p
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+functions"} algebras of functions
            by definition are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
            (over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} algebras
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebras"} Grassmann algebras
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℝ
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi q
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{O}(\mathbb{R}^{p\vert q})
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^p) \otimes_{\mathbb{R}} \wedge^\bullet (\mathbb{R}^\ast)^q
                  \\,.
          %p
            These serve as our “abstract super-coordinate systems” that define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            in direct analogy to how ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            serve as the abstract coordinate systems that define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            as found at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+coordinate+systems"} geometry of physics – coordinate systems
            and_
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
            %em .
          %p
            Then a general superspace is modeled as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            on the category of super Cartesian spaces, possibly satisfying some suitable properties. see remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ASheafAsASpace"}
            below for explanation of this perspective.
          %p
            This means that we follow the perspective of “
            %strong>
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"} functorial geometry
            ” due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Grothendieck65"} Grothendieck 65
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            is regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            over the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+schemes"} affine schemes
            (its “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+of+points"}> functor of points
            ”) satisfying the condition that it is covered by (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representables
            of) affines via
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+%C3%A9tale+morphism"} formally étale morphism
          %p
            Beware that – despite the urging in
            %a{:href => "#Grothendieck73"} Grothendieck 73
            that the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+spaces"} locally ringed spaces
            should be abandoned in favour of the perspective of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"} functorial geometry
            – most textbooks on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            do stick with the point of view of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+spaces"} locally ringed spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Grothendieck73"} Grothendieck’s urging
          %p
            An exception is the approach propagated in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schwarz84"} Schwarz 84
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Molotkov84"} Molotkov 84
            %a{:href => "KonechnySchwarz97"} Konechny-Schwarz 97
            of which a clean account is given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Sachse08"} Sachse 08
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            %a{:href => "#Grothendieck73"} Grothendieck’s urging
            half-way.
          %p
            The full application of the perspective of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"} functorial geometry
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            is known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+supergeometry"} synthetic differential supergeometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartesian+spaces"} super Cartesian spaces
            (
            %a{:href => "#Yetter88"}> Yetter 88, section 3
            ). This is essentially the perspective which we adopt here. We do however not refer to the (super-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axioms"} Kock-Lawvere axioms
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            but instead use the axiomatics of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"}> differential cohesion
            ” (
            %a{:href => "#Schreiber"}> Schreiber 13
            ). This we explain
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#SuperSmoothSets"} below
